# Local-ingest protocol (step 2, local lane): ${vault_tag}

You register the project's own evidence (${local_corpus}) in the hyperresearch vault, so that the
pipeline's agents and the catalogue agents can read and cite it like a fetched source.

- Working directory for every command: the project folder that holds the vault.
- Vault CLI: `${hpr}` (on Windows prefix commands with `PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8`).

## For each local source that evidences water tenure in ${area}

1. Extract the text with its locators intact: PDFs page by page with PyMuPDF (keep page markers;
   OCR only pages with no text layer, and say so); slide decks with python-pptx, slide numbers and
   speaker notes included; transcripts with line numbers; spreadsheets row by row.
2. Write a note body to `research/runs/${vault_tag}/temp/local/<slug>.md` with:
   - `Source file:` the absolute path of the original;
   - `Source type:` one of the water-tenure-analysis source types (interview, group-interview, fgd,
     workshop, admin-record, permit-register, statistics, grey-report, project-deliverable,
     derived-summary, academic, news, ...);
   - `Date:` of the event or document, not today;
   - `Author/issuer:` agency or team; respondents by role only, never by a private person's name;
   - `Locator scheme:` line, page, slide, row or timestamp;
   - `Reliability:` for example "machine transcript, not checked", "team deliverable derived from
     fieldwork round X", "official planning document";
   - `## Extract`: the tenure-relevant facts, EACH with a pinpoint (`l.212`, `p.34`, `slide 7`,
     `row 15`), verbatim quotes in the original language with a translation, numbers verbatim with
     units. Anchor every fact to a named place or point with its administrative and hydrological
     location and any coordinates the source gives. Transcribe tables of named users, intakes or
     permits row by row with the row pinpoint.
3. Create the note:

   ```
   ${hpr} note new "<Title (date)>" --body-file <scratch file> --tag ${vault_tag} --tag local-source
       --tag <lane-tag> --source "<absolute path>" --type source --tier <ground_truth|institutional|practitioner>
       --content-type <dataset|policy|report|article|forum> --summary "<one specific line>" -j
   ```

   If an option is rejected, drop it and retry; never drop `--tag` or `--source`.
4. Write claims to `research/runs/${vault_tag}/temp/claims-<note-id>.json` in the format the
   hyperresearch fetcher uses (claim, stance, evidence type, scope conditions, verbatim quoted
   support, numbers, entities, time period, region, confidence, source note id): 5 to 25 load-bearing
   claims per note.

## Exclusions (binding)

- AI-generated material is never evidence: chatbot transcripts, deep-research outputs, files named
  after an AI tool, machine-written validation or patch notes. Do not ingest them as sources. If one
  points to an original (a regulation, an article, an agency document), ingest the original if it is
  available, and list unresolved originals in your report.
- Wiki pages and summaries are digests: ingest the original they summarise; ingest the digest only
  when the original is unreachable, and then as `derived-summary`.
- Do not copy credentials, phone numbers or private persons' names.
- Material about other areas is skipped.

## Report back (300 words at most)

Note ids with one line each; files examined but skipped, and why; originals referred to but not
available; any table of named users or points you found (name and pinpoint only).
